From e61a363491923bbf2d748ede8a90108bee2d6907 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: baKon <mike.ski83@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 15:16:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] budget: an uneven world's cheaper rank pages the common
 tokens and keeps its experts

The resolver derives one split, the dearest rank's, for every card, and one
block table serves every rank. An attention-zero rank keeps no attention cache:
at TP3 on Flash-Next it pages the common 1.45M tokens in ~1.8 GiB of its
10.6 GiB KV pool, the rest is an address reservation nothing ever commits or
lends, and 1091 of its experts can never reach the card even with the cache
idle.

Such a rank now gets a KV pool that pages exactly the common token count (the
new KVManager::pool_mib_for_tokens, the inverse of paged_tokens, plus a 64 MiB
margin) and the difference in its weights pool, no further than every weight
resident. The per-rank split reaches BOTH readers of the resolved sizes --
Pools::configure and the planner -- so the grown pool is planned into. The
token count, the carve, the loan and the dearest ranks' pools are unchanged;
configure_kv names it if a re-sized pool ever binds.

Only a derived split under expert_tiered is re-split: a stated pool is the
operator's, and under auto a fully resident rank would choose all_vram while
its peers spill. Where every rank's cache costs the same -- every world the
heads divide -- no rank qualifies and every rank takes cfg_ as before.
